WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.678
Numeric input is a common source of subtle validation defects.

2
00:00:03.678 --> 00:00:09.195
A number can parse successfully and still be unsafe or meaningless for the intended use.

3
00:00:09.195 --> 00:00:15.815
Counts, offsets, indexes, lengths, capacities, timeouts, and allocation sizes all need domain checks before they influence program behavior.

4
00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:18.757
Signed and unsigned boundaries should be reviewed carefully.

5
00:00:18.757 --> 00:00:24.274
A negative value may become a very large value if it is converted without checking.

6
00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:28.687
A large unsigned value may not fit in a signed target type.

7
00:00:28.687 --> 00:00:33.468
A value that fits in one platform type may not fit in another.

8
00:00:33.468 --> 00:00:35.675
Validation should happen before narrowing conversions.

9
00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:41.928
Externally influenced sizes should be bounded before allocation, loop bounds, copy operations, indexing, slicing, and buffer growth.

10
00:00:41.928 --> 00:00:47.444
A count is not ready for use simply because it was represented as an integer.

11
00:00:47.444 --> 00:00:52.226
It must be within the range the program can process safely and reasonably.

12
00:00:52.226 --> 00:00:54.432
Derived calculations need overflow-aware thinking.

13
00:00:54.432 --> 00:01:04.362
If the program multiplies a record count by an item size, adds a header length, or converts units, the derived value may exceed the target type even

14
00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:07.305
when each input value looks acceptable by itself.

15
00:01:07.305 --> 00:01:09.144
Validate both inputs and results.

16
00:01:09.144 --> 00:01:14.293
Indexes and offsets should be checked against the actual container or record being used.

17
00:01:14.293 --> 00:01:20.545
An index that is valid for one array length may be invalid after filtering, decoding, or truncation.

18
00:01:20.545 --> 00:01:24.591
Keep the validated value close to the object it applies to.

19
00:01:24.591 --> 00:01:28.268
The review question is simple: what will this number control?

20
00:01:28.268 --> 00:01:35.624
If it controls memory, iteration, selection, timing, or resource use, validate the range, unit, type, conversion, and derived calculations before

21
00:01:35.624 --> 00:01:36.360
trusting it.

22
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:42.980
Use numeric parsing APIs that let the program distinguish successful conversion, out-of-range values, and leftover input.

23
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:49.600
Then preserve the checked type when possible instead of repeatedly converting the same raw value in different locations.
